+## Controllers, touch controls and the 1.43:1 view
+
+The iOS app fits the game into a centred 1.43:1 view (`dm_game_rect` in `front_ios.m`; an iPad's 2388×1668 screen is that shape, an iPhone gets bars at the sides) with the touch gamepad over the whole screen (`DmGamepad.m`). The Metal present keeps the game's own picture aspect inside the view: a Switch game's 16:9 picture is **letterboxed** in it, never stretched, and `dm_picture_aspect()` (`hle_touch.c`, set by `present_encode` / `present_encode_m4` in `nvn_mtl.m`) tells the touch mapping where that picture is. See "Aspect ratio" below for what filling the view takes.
+
+**Touch layout.** `dm_pad_unit` gives the layout unit `u`: `sqrt(width · height) / 633`, clamped to 0.8–1.6, scaled by `DM_PAD_SCALE`, and cut to `height / 330` and `width / 480` (the controls' extent in `u`), so nothing overlaps on any screen. `front_ios.m` uses it for the frame-rate label too. The left stick floats in games that stay docked (`dm_opmode() == 1`; those read no touch screen) and keeps its fixed ring in handheld-mode games, whose touches belong to the game (`DM_PAD_FLOAT=0/1` overrides).
+
+**Physical controllers** report through `dm_ctrl.h`: `DmController.m` (GameController, the default; `DM_PAD_BACKEND=auto|gc`) or `DmSDL3Pad.c` (`DM_PAD_BACKEND=sdl3`, only in an app built with SDL3). Buttons go through `dm_pad_face_buttons` (`dm_pad_core.c`): Xbox layout by default, `DM_PAD_FACE=nintendo` for the old positions. The touch gamepad keeps the Nintendo positions.
+
+- **Motion.** `GCMotion` (or SDL's gyro and accelerometer) becomes one sample per update in frame D (x right, y toward the top edge, z out of the face; acceleration as the force the sensor feels), then the axis map `DM_MOTION_AXES` (default `x,-y,-z`) takes it to the Switch frame, in which a controller lying flat, face up, reads (0, 0, -1) G (the rest value `hle_hid.c` has always used). `dm_pad_motion_set` hands it to the host, which integrates the angular velocity into the six-axis angle and direction (an exact rotation per step, rows re-orthonormalized) and falls back to the synthetic sensor 0.5 s after the last sample. The default map is derived from that rest value and the documented axes of GameController / SDL3, not measured on a device: with `DM_MOTION_LOG=1` the app logs a sample twice a second (frame D in, Switch frame out); lay the controller flat, face up, and expect acceleration (0, 0, -1) out, then tilt it right and forward and check the signs, and correct them with `DM_MOTION_AXES` (for example `x,y,-z`). The phone-shake sensor still adds its swing on top.
+- **Rumble.** While a controller with haptics is connected the host reports two vibration devices (left, right; `hle_hid.c`), `IsVibrationPermitted` is true, and each value goes to `DmController.m`'s sink, which layers the low band and the high band on two continuous haptic events per handle (intensity from the amplitude, sharpness from the frequency on a log scale over 40–1252 Hz) and stops them after a second of silence. A controller with a single haptics locality gets both sides mixed (the stronger of the two per band). Without a controller nothing is reported, as before. The DualSense's speaker is not an audio output for an iOS app (nothing over Bluetooth, and GameController's haptics are actuator-only), so it is not driven. The SDL3 backend maps the low band to SDL's low-frequency motor and the high band to its high-frequency one (no layering), so GameController is the better backend for a DualSense.
+- **SDL3** is optional and off by default (`DmSDL3Pad.c` is two stubs without `DM_HAVE_SDL3`). Build it from an extracted SDL3 source tree (3.2 or newer; nothing is downloaded): `dm/ios/tools/build_sdl3.sh <SDL source> [prefix]`, then `SDL3_PREFIX=<prefix> … dm/ios/tools/build_ipa.sh`. SDL's HIDAPI drivers are off (they would use CoreBluetooth, which the app has no usage description for): controllers come through SDL's GameController driver, so it sees the same devices; what it adds is SDL's gamepad API. The backend was written against SDL3's public API and has been syntax-checked only against a stub of it: build it once and run it before relying on it.
+- **Tests** (`make -C dm/host/tests GAME=<id> run`): `t_pad_core` (face layouts, axis maps, rumble mapping) and `t_hid_pad`, which includes `hle_hid.c` itself and checks the vibration handles / info / values, the sink, and the six-axis state from a controller's motion against hand-computed rotations.
+
+**Aspect ratio.** A game draws its own picture at its own aspect (a Switch game: 16:9) and lays out its HUD for it, so a 1.43:1 view shows the picture with bars above and below. Stretching it to fill (what an earlier build did) makes circles into ovals; cropping the sides cuts the HUD's corners. Filling the view without either takes the game's own projection and layout to use the new ratio, which is a patch to the game's code, per game and per build (`CUTS` in `patch_nso.py`, keyed by build ID). `dm/tools/aspect_scan.py <main NSO> --ratio 1.43` finds where the code builds 16/9 (MOVZ/MOVK pairs, with the literal-pool copies) and prints each pair as a `CUTS` entry with the old bytes (the rewriter checks them) and the new ones. It cannot say which hit is the camera and which the HUD: add one candidate at a time, run the rewriter and a build, and look at the picture (a projection hit widens or heightens the 3D view without squeezing it; a layout hit moves HUD elements). No such patch exists in the repo yet, because it needs the game's binary.
